{
  "slug": "artist:mos-def",
  "name": "Mos Def",
  "editorial_article": "Born Dante Terrell Smith in 1973 Brooklyn, Mos Def first gained widespread attention in 1996 by forming the duo Black Star alongside Talib Kweli. Their 1998 debut, \"Mos Def & Talib Kweli Are Black Star,\" featured the production work of Hi-Tek and became a pillar of conscious hip-hop. The following year, he released his solo debut \"Black on Both Sides\" through Rawkus Records, an album defined by tracks like \"Ms. Fat Booty.\" Throughout the 2000s, his career spanned eclectic territory, from contributing to the 2009 \"Blakroc\" project with The Black Keys and Damon Dash to his work on the Gorillaz hit \"Stylo\" alongside Bobby Womack. He is also a celebrated actor, having received an Emmy nomination for his portrayal of Vivien Thomas in the 2004 HBO film \"Something the Lord Made.\" In 2011, the artist declared he had changed his name to Yasiin Bey, an identity he had used privately since 1999.",
